# PART 12 — ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The implementation is not complete until all applicable criteria pass.

### Gateway management

- All five initial gateway types are represented.
- Stripe and PayPal have separate test/live configurations.
- Bank Transfer and COD have correct manual settlement workflows.
- Test Gateway is restricted to test environments.
- Credentials are encrypted.
- Super Admin authorization is enforced server-side.

### Payments

- Provider payment initiation works in sandbox.
- Provider verification is server-side.
- Invalid webhook signatures are rejected.
- Duplicate events cannot process a payment twice.
- Payment amounts and currencies are validated.
- Inventory is committed exactly once.

### Refunds

- Full refunds work.
- Partial refunds work.
- Over-refunds are rejected.
- Duplicate requests are idempotent.
- Unknown outcomes are not blindly retried.
- Provider references are persisted.
- Reconciliation recovers verified provider states.
- Database locks are not held during external HTTP calls.

### Isolation

- Test transactions never enter live reporting.
- Test payments never trigger live fulfillment.
- Test refunds cannot affect live payments.
- Live activation is restricted to Super Admin.

### Verification

- Every PHPUnit and feature test uses MySQL.
- Dedicated database is `fashion_ecommerce_test`.
- Full Laravel suite passes on MySQL.
- Concurrency tests use independent processes against MySQL.
- Actual database engine and database name are reported.
- No destructive test operation can touch development or production.

### Production readiness

Do not declare production-ready until genuine provider sandbox tests, webhook verification, refund recovery, credential security and operational procedures are verified.

Merchant eligibility and live settlement must be independently confirmed.

